### QubicaCinema — a customer books seats, end to end.
###
### Run the requests top to bottom (VS Code "REST Client", or the HTTP client built into Rider).
### Each one reads what it needs from an earlier response, so nothing has to be copied by hand.
###
### Aspire assigns the gateway's port on every run: read the `gateway` endpoint in the dashboard and put it here.
@gateway = http://localhost:5100

# The customer used below. Registering the same address twice is a 409, which is correct: on a second run,
# skip the register request and go straight to the login.
@email = ada@example.com
@password = Correct-horse-1


### 1. Register a customer. There is no way to register as an administrator.
POST {{gateway}}/api/v1/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}",
  "firstName": "Ada",
  "lastName": "Lovelace"
}


### 2. Log in. The token is used by every request that follows.
# @name login
POST {{gateway}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}


### 3. The programme. Public: no token needed. The first screening is used below.
# @name programme
@token = {{login.response.body.$.accessToken}}
GET {{gateway}}/api/v1/screenings?pageSize=5


### 4. The seat map of that screening. Cache-Control: no-store — it is a snapshot, not a hold.
# @name seatMap
@screeningId = {{programme.response.body.$.items[0].id}}
GET {{gateway}}/api/v1/screenings/{{screeningId}}/seats


### 5. Book two chosen seats. The Idempotency-Key is generated once per logical request: sending the same
### key and body again returns this response instead of booking twice. (Rider spells the variable {{$uuid}}.)
# @name bookBySeat
@seatA = {{seatMap.response.body.$.seats[0].seatId}}
@seatB = {{seatMap.response.body.$.seats[1].seatId}}
POST {{gateway}}/api/v1/bookings
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{$guid}}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "screeningId": "{{screeningId}}",
      "selection": { "mode": "seats", "seatIds": ["{{seatA}}", "{{seatB}}"] }
    }
  ]
}


### 6. Book by quantity: ask for three seats and let the system allocate them side by side.
@bookingId = {{bookBySeat.response.body.$.id}}
POST {{gateway}}/api/v1/bookings
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{$guid}}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "screeningId": "{{screeningId}}",
      "selection": { "mode": "quantity", "quantity": 3 }
    }
  ]
}


### 7. Try to book the first seat again. Expect 409, with the seat ids that were refused in `unavailableSeatIds`.
POST {{gateway}}/api/v1/bookings
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{$guid}}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "screeningId": "{{screeningId}}",
      "selection": { "mode": "seats", "seatIds": ["{{seatA}}"] }
    }
  ]
}


### 8. Read the booking back. Note its ETag.
GET {{gateway}}/api/v1/bookings/{{bookingId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 9. Give back one seat. Cancelling the last seat of a booking cancels the booking.
### (A cancellation is a POST to a sub-resource, not a DELETE: the booking stays readable afterwards.)
POST {{gateway}}/api/v1/bookings/{{bookingId}}/items/{{bookBySeat.response.body.$.items[0].id}}/cancellation
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 10. Cancel what is left of the booking. Asking twice is not an error.
POST {{gateway}}/api/v1/bookings/{{bookingId}}/cancellation
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 11. Read it again: status Cancelled, and the seats are free on the seat map once more.
GET {{gateway}}/api/v1/bookings/{{bookingId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
